            <td height="209"><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> 
              PMWATCH - April 10, 2002 -- Anyone who has been following US media 
              coverage of the Palestinian-Israeli conflict, whether since this 
              latest onslaught against Palestinian towns and villages, or since 
              the outbreak of the Al-Aqsa Intifada, or since the first Intifada, 
              or even before, could not but have been struck by the fact that 
              the US media, whether in its news coverage or in its commentary, 
              has virtually ignored what respectable, reliable, and trustworthy 
              human rights organizations have had to say about what is taking 
              place on the ground. Indeed, it seems that journalists on the ground 
              rely more on what comes out of the Israeli Prime Minister's office, 
              or the office of the Foreign Minister, than it does on what comes 
              out of Amnesty International, Human Rights Watch, or even Israeli 
              human rights groups such as Gush Shalom or Peace Now. </font> 
              <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> Please impress 
                upon the media the need to include findings published by respectable 
                and reputable human rights organizations.</font> 
              </p><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif">Go to any 
                of the following sites, copy an article, a press release, or any 
                item of interest, paste it in the space for "Your letter" 
                below and press send.</font> 
